WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
As a movie based on an attraction in Disneyland,&nbsp;
"Jungle Cruise" is chock full of Easter eggs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.480
other small nods to the ride's Adventureland&nbsp;
home. Here are some you may have missed.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.920
A fictional character in the Jungle&nbsp;
Cruise/Adventureland universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.320
Dr. Albert Falls was introduced in the attraction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.203
as a joke - the namesake of the famous&nbsp;
Schweitzer Falls, which he "discovered."

00:00:18.203 --> 00:00:23.040
Albert Falls' characterization grew with the&nbsp;
expansion of the Jungle Cruise backstory in 2015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.080
Now, Albert's books and other items&nbsp;
found on his travels are featured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.960
on shelves in the Skipper Canteen&nbsp;
restaurant, and there's a bronze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.040
bust of the famed fictional explorer in&nbsp;
Trader Sam's at the Disneyland Hotel.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.600
In the "Jungle Cruise" film, Dr. Albert Falls is&nbsp;
an offscreen character whose travel documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.680
maps, and an arrowhead are sought after&nbsp;
by Dr. Lily Houghton and Prince Joachim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.720
in their search for the coveted healing&nbsp;
tree. In the beginning of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.280
Lily breaks into and snoops around an English&nbsp;
archaeological society's archives and finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.600
the arrow she's looking for inside a crate&nbsp;
labeled with "Dr. A. Falls." Albert Falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.120
is also briefly mentioned a couple of other&nbsp;
times in the film when Lily and Frank are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.880
discussing the exploration of the Amazon&nbsp;
and the search for the "tears of the moon."

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.400
Founded by the aforementioned Dr. Albert Falls,&nbsp;
the Jungle Navigation Company Ltd. is a fictional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.000
business set within the Adventureland&nbsp;
and Jungle Cruise world of Disney parks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.760
It's the parent company and operator of the&nbsp;
Jungle Cruise tours of the world's rivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.240
and also boasts the popular Skipper Canteen mess&nbsp;
hall-style eatery. The company was introduced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.200
Disneyland's and Magic Kingdom's Adventureland&nbsp;
as part of updates to the parks in the 1990s.&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.840
The Jungle Navigation Company's Skipper Canteen&nbsp;
menu is also full of nods to the Jungle Cruise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.400
attraction, including dishes named after Albert&nbsp;
Falls and the attraction's boats. There's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.120
a "Not Piranha" sustainable fish dish, which is&nbsp;
made all the more funny after seeing Frank serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.680
up fresh piranha in the "Jungle Cruise" movie.
In the film, a sign bearing the Jungle Navigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.680
Company Ltd. logo can be seen at the docks&nbsp;
as Frank tries to get out of paying Nilo.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.600
Fans of Disneyland's Enchanted&nbsp;
Tiki Room were likely thrilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.600
to see a brief but memorable "Jungle&nbsp;
Cruise" cameo by Rosita the cockatoo.
 &nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.080
The white-feathered Rosita is one of the "showgirl&nbsp;
birds" who appear in the attraction and sing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.640
"Let's All Sing Like the Birdies Sing." However,&nbsp;
Rosita's spot on the mobile is empty because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.880
left the group for adventures elsewhere. She&nbsp;
now inhabits various spots around Disneyland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.960
including the Tropical Hideaway and a&nbsp;
riverside cage on the Jungle Cruise attraction.&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.840
In the "Jungle Cruise" film,&nbsp;
Paul Giamatti's character, Nilo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.560
has a pet cockatoo named Rosita, who repeatedly&nbsp;
squawks the memorable line "Frank owes me money."
 &nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.200
It's the eighth wonder of the world and one of&nbsp;
the greatest continuous jokes in theme park ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.840
history: the backside of water. Jungle Cruise ride&nbsp;
fans will laugh at this joke no matter how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.520
times they've heard it and seen the other side&nbsp;
of the famous Schweitzer Falls. Boat skippers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.800
tell passengers they like to call it "O2H."
In the "Behind the Attraction" series on Disney+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.720
imagineers behind the Jungle Cruise ride and&nbsp;
some longtime skippers said the backside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.320
water joke is an essential part of the&nbsp;
experience. If the joke isn't told,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.800
guests will remind the skippers of their misstep.
Likewise, including the backside of water joke was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.880
a must for the "Jungle Cruise" film. It was&nbsp;
featured prominently in the first trailer:&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:03:00.000
"Ladies and gentlemen, the moment you've&nbsp;
been waiting for ... the backside of water."&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.000
That joke, and all the others Frank deadpans,&nbsp;
elicit groans and eye rolls from the tourists.&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
In a similar nod to the human-made river cruise&nbsp;
at Disneyland, the character Frank is shown to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.520
have rigged many of the tricks in the water&nbsp;
and along the shore to give his boat tour a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.000
more adventurous feel. To create the backside of&nbsp;
water, he put together a system of water troughs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.000
and pulleys to produce a small waterfall at&nbsp;
the best moment to deliver the one-liner.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.800
The Jungle Cruise ride we know and love today&nbsp;
isn't the same one that ferried passengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.360
along the world's rivers on Disneyland's&nbsp;
opening day in 1955. For almost a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.536
nature and wildlife education was the focus&nbsp;
of the original Jungle Cruise attraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.536 --> 00:03:39.928
The corny "dad jokes" and dry humor didn't&nbsp;
make it into the ride until the 1960s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:43.600
Alongside the backside of water, skippers&nbsp;
now make jokes about a group of lions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.840
eating a zebra "on the rocks" and sharing&nbsp;
the meal "with pride." There's also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.480
Nile crocodile named Ginger who ... snaps.&nbsp;
The skippers call her "one tough cookie."&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:59.201
Johnson's character Frank really leans into the&nbsp;
punny skipper role in "Jungle Cruise." When he&nbsp;gestures to some sandstone rocks, Frank says&nbsp;

00:03:59.201 --> 00:04:04.737
 
"The rocks you see here are sandstone. But&nbsp;some people just take them for granite." 

00:04:04.737 --> 00:04:06.800
 
 
As Frank continues, his audience's groans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.920
and eye rolls grow in intensity. He even prompts&nbsp;
a silent "wow" from one passenger after saying he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.640
got "canned" from his previous job at an orange&nbsp;
juice factory because "I couldn't concentrate."&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.080
Later in the movie, when Frank, Lily and&nbsp;
McGregor are being taken to the village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:23.760
of native "headhunters," Frank says&nbsp;
it's not a great place to "be headed."

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.960
The Jungle Cruise wouldn't be the Jungle Cruise&nbsp;
without its animatronic animals, especially the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.200
hippos. In the Disneyland ride, the boat slowly&nbsp;
moves through the hippo pool while the skipper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.760
tells the passengers to stay still so as not to&nbsp;
rock the boat and disturb the massive animals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.840
At one point, the skipper "fires" a fake pistol&nbsp;
to scare some of the hippos away from the boat.&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:44.880
A brief but similar scene happens&nbsp;
in the "Jungle Cruise" movie. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.480
Frank narrates the many ways the Amazon&nbsp;
jungle has killed previous explorers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.080
the boat rumbles past a stationary&nbsp;
fake hippo protruding from the water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.000
Then, as Frank is activating a bunch of his&nbsp;
tricks to entertain the passengers, he uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.480
a pistol kept near the boat's wheel to fire at&nbsp;
a rope and unleash the mechanical roaring hippo.&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.040
Hippos aren't native to the Amazon region - a&nbsp;
fact pointed out by a young girl on the boat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.240
whom Frank shushes - so they aren't&nbsp;
seen again in the rest of the film.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.760
At the beginning of 2021, the Jungle Cruise&nbsp;
ride received some major storytelling updates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.040
to get rid of "negative depictions of&nbsp;
native people," which included getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.719
rid of the unofficial Jungle Cruise&nbsp;
mascot, the Trader Sam animatronic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:24.880
Though Trader Sam had different animatronic&nbsp;
iterations in Disneyland and Magic Kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.434
the character was described by critics as a&nbsp;
racist caricature of Indigenous African peoples.&nbsp;

00:05:29.434 --> 00:05:33.760
Trader Sam got another revamp in the "Jungle&nbsp;
Cruise" movie. In the film, Veronica Falcón&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:38.080
portrays Trader Sam as the leader of the tribe&nbsp;
of Indigenous people who guard the healing tree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.960
She makes several quips about "working" with Frank&nbsp;
in playing outdated stereotypes of native peoples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.201
for his river cruise story and how she makes&nbsp;
money off of colonial notions of her people.&nbsp;

00:05:47.201 --> 00:05:50.800
The portrayal of Trader Sam and her people&nbsp;
doesn't always hit the nail on the head when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.600
it comes to reimagining the character. There is a&nbsp;
moment when Trader Sam dons Lily's brother's top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.000
hat in a small nod to the Magic Kingdom version&nbsp;
of Trader Sam, who's not only holding several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.560
shrunken heads but also wearing one as a necklace.&nbsp;
But critics have said the new Trader Sam is a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.000
quote, "hearty attempt to reimagine a&nbsp;
less racist version of the character."
 
 &nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.640
This one may be a bit of a stretch, but&nbsp;
Prince Joachim's submarine is strangely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.746
reminiscent of the Nautilus vessel from&nbsp;
Disney's "20,000 Leagues Under the Sea."&nbsp;

00:06:16.746 --> 00:06:21.280
Though the interior of the submarine is cold and&nbsp;
cramped, Joachim somehow also has extravagant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.919
luxurious quarters that solidify his&nbsp;
bumbling caricature of a colonizing villain.&nbsp;

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:31.200
The famous Nautilus submarine was created by&nbsp;
Jules Verne and featured in his 1870 novel "20,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.400
Leagues Under the Sea" and in the Disney film of&nbsp;
the same name from 1954. The Nautilus is shaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.200
like a fish and has a shingled ramming fern at the&nbsp;
bow. Joachim's submarine boasts a similar serrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.320
ramming fern at its bow, but so did many other&nbsp;
German UB-II-type submarines during World War I.&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.200
Magic Kingdom at Disney World had a 20,000 Leagues&nbsp;
Under the Sea attraction that featured a 20-minute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.920
submarine ride aboard an adapted version of&nbsp;
Nautilus, but the ride was shuttered in 1994.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.840
The 1951 adventure classic "The African Queen" is&nbsp;
one of the key elements that inspired the layout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.400
and storytelling of the original Jungle Cruise&nbsp;
boat ride in Disneyland. Seventy years later, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:11.680
inspiration lives on in the "Jungle Cruise" film.
The film is based on the 1935 novel of the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.920
name and stars Humphrey Bogart as a skipper&nbsp;
ferrying a woman played by Katharine Hepburn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.920
down a river in a boat named the African&nbsp;
Queen. The movie is also set during World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.600
War I and has long been heralded by&nbsp;
critics as thrilling, adventurous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.760
and funny with a hint of lighthearted romance.
While "Jungle Cruise" is obviously based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.840
the theme park attraction, it clearly also&nbsp;
takes inspiration from "The African Queen."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:36.000
Looper's review of the film said Johnson's Frank&nbsp;
"looks like the human growth hormone version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.639
of Bogart" with Blunt's character giving "her&nbsp;
ambitious scientist a little glint of Hepburn."&nbsp;

00:07:40.639 --> 00:07:44.720
Frank also sports a nearly identical&nbsp;
skipper outfit to Bogart's Charlie Allnut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.240
complete with a red scarf tied around&nbsp;
his neck and a black-billed white cap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.040
The design of Frank's creaky, hodgepodge riverboat&nbsp;
also pays homage to Charlie's African Queen.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.880
Though "Jungle Cruise" is set in 1916, it does&nbsp;
have a nod to "Disney's True-Life Adventure"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:04.080
nature documentaries, which premiered in the&nbsp;
late 1940s, ran through 1960, and inspired the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.523
original Jungle Cruise attraction at Disneyland.
During a quieter moment in "Jungle Cruise,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.523 --> 00:08:12.720
Blunt's character Lily uses a box camera to&nbsp;
capture motion pictures of the wildlife and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.720
scenery of the jungle while sailing down&nbsp;
the Amazon River. She captures soundless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:21.520
black and white shots of butterflies, birds,&nbsp;
and foliage while Johnson's Frank stares in awe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:25.601
He's never seen a motion picture camera&nbsp;
before, so Lily lets him try it out.&nbsp;

00:08:25.601 --> 00:08:28.800
Lily also explains the excitement of&nbsp;
motion pictures and how they can bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.280
any place in the world right to you. It's a&nbsp;
similar sentiment shared by Walt Disney and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:36.640
his team when shooting and sharing the&nbsp;
"True-Life Adventure" features as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.880
as creating the Jungle Cruise ride. Both the&nbsp;
feature and attraction were made to bring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.280
world to an audience — and give that audience&nbsp;
a greater appreciation for wildlife and nature.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.240
Yes, veteran heavy metal rockers Metallica&nbsp;
reworked an orchestral version of "Nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.634
Else Matters" for a key scene in&nbsp;
Disney's "Jungle Cruise" movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.634 --> 00:08:57.760
It's an unexpected but delightful detail that&nbsp;
somehow fits in a flashback scene set in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:03.280
mid-1500s with Spanish conquistadors and a tribe&nbsp;
of Indigenous people in the Amazonian rainforest.&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:08.000
Metallica drummer Lars Ulrich talked about&nbsp;
the Disney collaboration in September 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.560
saying it goes back to "lifelong rock fan" Sean&nbsp;
Bailey, who is Disney's production president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.120
Ulrich worked with "Jungle Cruise" score composer&nbsp;
James Newton Howard to rearrange "Nothing Else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.360
Matters" into a piece that's instrumental&nbsp;
to the dark, frenzied scene. In an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.901
with Collider, Ulrich said the version is&nbsp;
a "very unusual" morph of the iconic song.&nbsp;

00:09:25.901 --> 00:09:29.840
The flashback scene is quick and feels a bit&nbsp;
jammed with a lot of plot points that can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.680
hard to follow, but the new arrangement&nbsp;
of "Nothing Else Matters" is a dizzying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.580
welcome addition to a score otherwise&nbsp;
filled with spirited adventure ballads.&nbsp;

